var e={kindLabels:{"press-release":`Press Release`,"founder-note":`Founder Note`},posts:[{slug:`a-year-in-view-the-facts-had-to-hold`,ogImage:`/og/news-a-year-in-view-the-facts-had-to-hold.png`,ogEyebrow:`A Year in View`,ogTitleParts:[`The models kept changing.`,`The facts had to hold.`],ogSubtitle:`Eason Wang on what changed in AI answers, models, and our product in year one, and 5 predictions for year two.`,kind:`founder-note`,title:`A Year in View: The Models Kept Changing. The Facts Had to Hold.`,metaTitle:`A Year in View: The Models Kept Changing. The Facts Had to Hold.`,description:`Eason Wang on year one of answer engine optimization: how buyers, engines, and models changed, what we built and deleted, and 5 predictions for year two.`,date:`2026-09-26`,datePublished:`2026-09-26`,updated:`2026-09-26`,tag:`Year One`,author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`},relatedSlugs:[`your-brand-ai-ready-one-year-of-solcrys`,`introducing-the-corporate-context-engine`],body:[{type:`paragraph`,text:`Gwen wrote about [why we started and whom we listened to](/news/your-brand-ai-ready-one-year-of-solcrys/). This note is the other half of year one: what changed underneath us. Three things moved at once over the past 12 months. Buyers moved into AI answers. The models we build on became cheaper and more capable nearly every month. And our product changed shape several times in response, sometimes by adding things and sometimes by deleting them.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`If I had to compress the year into one sentence, it would be this: generation got cheap and models became replaceable parts, so the scarce inputs became the facts a brand can stand behind and the evidence that something actually worked. What follows is how we got there, organized around the questions customers asked us, in their words, roughly in the order they asked them.`},{type:`heading`,text:`1. “What is AI saying about us?”`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`When we started in September 2025, buyers were already ahead of the brands they were evaluating. In Forrester's [State of Business Buying 2026](https://www.forrester.com/blogs/state-of-business-buying-2026/), 94% of business buyers said they use AI somewhere in their buying process. [G2 found](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/new-g2-research-half-of-b2b-software-buyers-now-start-their-research-with-ai-chatbots-302742807.html) that 51% of B2B software buyers now start their research in an AI chatbot more often than in Google, up from 29% in April 2025. At the same time, the clicks that used to tell marketers what was happening started falling away. Chartbeat data in the [Reuters Institute's 2026 trends report](https://reutersinstitute.politics.ox.ac.uk/journalism-media-and-technology-trends-and-predictions-2026) shows Google organic referrals to news sites falling 33% worldwide and 38% in the U.S. in the year to November 2025.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`So the first question was simple, and almost nobody could answer it: what does AI say about us? The technology to answer it arrived just in time. On September 29, 2025, Anthropic released Claude Sonnet 4.5, renamed its Claude Code SDK the Claude Agent SDK, and published an engineering essay that helped give the year its vocabulary: [context engineering](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents), the idea that what a model reads matters as much as how you ask it. Search-grounded model APIs made it practical to put a buyer's question to an engine, over and over, and record what came back.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Two lessons from those months still shape the product. The first is that answers are not stable. Ask the same question twice and you get a different list. [SparkToro's study](https://sparktoro.com/blog/new-research-ais-are-highly-inconsistent-when-recommending-brands-or-products-marketers-should-take-care-when-tracking-ai-visibility/) of 2,961 runs later put a number on it: you would wait more than 1,000 runs to see two brand lists come back in the same order. The same study concluded that visibility measured as a rate across many runs is a reasonable metric, and that a "ranking position in AI" is not. We had reached the same conclusion from our own data, which is why our core metrics are rates across repeated runs, not the rank in a single answer.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The second is that people want to use this where they already work. We built our first MCP server in October 2025, before we had an AEO product and 2 months before MCP moved into the Linux Foundation's new [Agentic AI Foundation](https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2025-12-09-mcp-joins-agentic-ai-foundation/). By July 2026, the protocol's main SDKs were [approaching half a billion downloads a month](https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2026-07-28/). MCP is now [how customers bring SolCrys into Claude, ChatGPT, and Cursor](/why-we-bet-on-mcp/), and it can act, not just read: since June, a customer's own assistant can mark an Action Hub task as published, through a separate permission they opt into.`},{type:`heading`,text:`2. “What do we do about it?”`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`By early 2026 the category had a name. In March, Gartner published its first [Market Guide for Answer Engine Visibility Tools](https://www.gartner.com/en/documents/7559273). The name was accurate about where the market stood: the tools were built to see. In May, [Google's own guidance](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/ai-optimization-guide) gave Google's answer to a debate that had run all year: "optimizing for generative AI search is optimizing for the search experience, and thus still SEO." For Google's AI surfaces, answer engine optimization is an operating layer on top of SEO foundations, not a replacement for them.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Seeing a gap is not the same as closing it, and closing it became the next question. The model side moved fastest here. In February, Claude Opus 4.6 arrived with a million-token context window in beta, and OpenAI described an internal project in which a small team [shipped about a million lines of code in 5 months without writing any of it by hand](https://openai.com/index/harness-engineering/). They called the discipline harness engineering. In April, Anthropic put the lesson in one sentence: "[Harnesses encode assumptions that go stale as models improve](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/managed-agents)." The model does the work. The harness decides what it reads, what it is allowed to do, and how its work gets checked, and it has to be rebuilt as the models change.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That is the shape we gave the product. In May we launched SolCrys AEO as a loop, Measure → Diagnose → Execute → Verify, instead of a dashboard. Measure and Diagnose were the parts we already knew how to build. Execute had to be governed: drafts grounded in a company's approved facts, and a person approving anything before it ships. Verify turned out to be the hardest verb in the loop.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Why we deleted our first Verify`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Our first verification system re-checked published pages and reported impact at 7, 14, and 30 days. The numbers looked clean. But it had no control prompts and no confidence intervals, and our internal review in May concluded that we could not defend the headline numbers. We removed it that month. What replaced it is deliberately narrower: re-run the same frozen prompt set on the same engines, re-audit the same page to see whether its score recovered, and treat any before-and-after number as an observation, not proof of cause.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`This matters beyond our product. In July, a [survey of 45 GEO studies](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.14035) concluded that "no reviewed technique shows a stable, longitudinal, cross-platform causal effect." That doesn't mean nothing works. It means the burden of proof sits with whoever claims a result, including us. A lift shown without a sample size is a picture, not a finding.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`The harness we use on ourselves`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`We build SolCrys the way we think marketing will increasingly run: agents do much of the work inside a harness that people design. We merged about 1,900 pull requests in our first year, and in August and September close to 9 in 10 were marked as written with a coding agent. The human work moved to specs, evaluations, and review, with a second model from a different family often reviewing alongside. The discipline we sell is the one we apply to our own code: approved context, governed execution, and honest verification.`},{type:`heading`,text:`3. “Is it right, and does it choose us?”`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Once teams could see how they appeared, they asked harder questions: is what AI says about us true, and does being mentioned mean being chosen? The honest answer to both: not reliably. [The same G2 survey](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/new-g2-research-half-of-b2b-software-buyers-now-start-their-research-with-ai-chatbots-302742807.html) found that 64% of software buyers get inaccurate recommendations from AI chatbots often or very often. [A study of Google AI Overviews](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.14021) by Xu, Iqbal, and Montgomery found that 11.0% of 98,020 claims were not supported by the pages cited for them. And [Lily Ray's analysis](https://searchengineland.com/google-ai-overviews-cite-self-serving-listicles-recommend-competitors-480573) of B2B software queries found that when AI Overviews cited a brand's own "best of" listicle, 224 of 323 times the brand itself was left out of the recommendation. Being a source and being the answer are different things.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Accuracy also started to look like a legal question. In May, the [Regional Court of Munich](https://the-decoder.com/landmark-german-ruling-declares-googles-ai-overviews-are-googles-own-words-and-makes-it-liable-for-false-answers/) held that Google's AI Overviews are Google's own content, not a list of third-party results, and that Google is liable for false statements in them. The ruling is not final, and Google has said it is reviewing it. The direction is still worth noticing: what an engine says about your company is starting to count as something someone said.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The technology change that let us answer these questions was context length. In January our knowledge pipeline still worked the old way: embed documents, retrieve the closest fragments, and hope the right fragment came back. By June, context windows were large and cheap enough that we stopped retrieving fragments of a company's truth. Answer Accuracy grades each AI answer against the company's entire Corporate Context, and it cannot mark a claim wrong without pointing to the evidence. [Recommendation Score](/ai-recommendation-score/) measures how favorably an engine endorses a brand against its competitors, and every point traces back to quoted text in the answer.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That is why the [Corporate Context Engine](/news/introducing-the-corporate-context-engine/) sits underneath everything we shipped after it. Engineers spent the year learning that the model matters less than what it reads. For a brand, what the model reads is its approved facts, claims, and proof. Keep those current and consistent, and every output built on them, from an AI answer to a webpage to a booth, inherits the accuracy. Let them drift, and no model upgrade will fix it.`},{type:`heading`,text:`4. What holds when the ground moves`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`August put that to the test. In the second week of the month, ChatGPT all but stopped citing Reddit. Independent trackers, [as reported by Search Engine Journal](https://www.searchenginejournal.com/why-reddits-chatgpt-citation-drop-isnt-fully-explained/586479/), measured Reddit's share of ChatGPT citations falling from 3.83% to 0.52% within about a week. In our own tracking across 5 engines, the break was ChatGPT's alone: Google AI Overviews, Perplexity, and Gemini barely moved, and the citations ChatGPT dropped were replaced mostly by vendors' official sites, documentation, and government pages.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`It had happened before. The same report notes that in September 2025, several trackers saw Reddit's share of ChatGPT citations collapse within a few weeks. Two drops in a year, on one engine, with no change in what Reddit users were writing, tell you what kind of variable this is. Which sources an engine names is decided at least as much by licensing, retrieval policy, and cost as by the quality of the content. We changed our own stance to match: we now treat Reddit as a place engines read to learn what customers think, rather than a citation to be won.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`It also showed why measurement has to follow the surface buyers use. In our own side-by-side runs, ChatGPT's developer API and the ChatGPT app did not cite the same sources, so a team measuring only through the API sees a different citation picture from the one its buyers see. We moved more of our own measurement closer to the answers buyers actually see, with each prompt sent the way a person would type it.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Agentic commerce had a similar year. OpenAI launched Instant Checkout in ChatGPT in September 2025 and [phased it out in March 2026](https://www.digitalcommerce360.com/2026/03/24/openai-agentic-commerce-updates-chatgpt-walmart/), after [Walmart reported](https://searchengineland.com/walmart-chatgpt-checkout-converted-worse-472071) that purchases made inside ChatGPT converted at one-third the rate of click-outs to its own site. OpenAI [shut down its Atlas browser](https://9to5mac.com/2026/08/04/openai-explains-what-will-happen-when-chatgpt-atlas-shuts-down-this-weekend/) on August 9. And only 9% of B2B software buyers told [G2](https://company.g2.com/news/buyer-behavior-2026) they were comfortable letting an agent execute purchases, even within approved guardrails. For now, discovery increasingly happens in AI, and most decisions still close on the brand's own surface. That is a large part of why we started building webpages with customers like [Cornelis](/customers/cornelis/): the page an engine cites and the page a buyer lands on should tell the same story, in the same words.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The models moved just as fast. Anthropic alone shipped Claude Opus 5, Fable 5.1, and Opus 5.5 between late July and late September, and the price of its Opus tier fell from $15 per million input tokens for Opus 4.1 a year earlier to [$4 for Opus 5.5](https://www.anthropic.com/claude-opus-5-5) this week. Cheaper models are good news. But for a measurement product, every model change is a migration. When we moved to a new model generation in July, several things that had worked the day before broke. So we built the unglamorous parts: evaluation sets labeled in 2 independent blind passes with disagreements adjudicated, gates a new model has to pass before it takes over a job, and the freedom to switch providers without rewriting our code. And one rule we don't bend: we never substitute the model that queries an engine, because that would change the thing being measured.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`[Signals](/signal-weekly-market-brief/), which we added in August to brief customers on their industry and competitors, taught us a related lesson. We first filtered candidate stories with a score threshold and a confidence floor. On one 40-candidate scan, everything worth reading scored between 29 and 36 and the noise between 2 and 8, so the ordering was right, yet the threshold threw away 3 of the top 4 items. The model's self-reported confidence ran backwards: the items worth reading averaged 60, the noise 97. We now rank instead of thresholding, and confidence is a label rather than a gate. When a model's scores are ordered but not calibrated, rank them.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`So what holds? The layer you own: your facts, stated the same way everywhere and corroborated by sources you don't control. Citations move with policy, and models turn over every few months. A company's approved facts are the one part that should change only when someone decides to change them.`},{type:`heading`,text:`What I got right, and what I got wrong`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`I published several positions on this site during the year. Here is how they held up.`},{type:`bullets`,items:[`[Production is cheap, trust is scarce](/production-is-cheap-trust-is-scarce/): **held, and sharpened.** Generation kept getting cheaper while the answer itself became paid, contested ground: ChatGPT's ads business passed a [$1 billion annual run rate](https://digiday.com/media-buying/openais-chatgpt-ads-business-hits-1-billion-run-rate-as-europe-gets-self-serve-access/) in under 200 days.`,`**The same essay's figure that Wikipedia and Reddit drive more than a quarter of ChatGPT's U.S. citations: out of date.** It came from January and February 2026 data. Since August it no longer holds on ChatGPT, and the page now carries a dated correction.`,`[AI cites consensus, not authority](/ai-citations-consensus-not-authority/): **needs a correction.** Corroboration across independent sources still matters. But after August, ChatGPT leaned toward official pages and documentation, so on that engine a company's own canonical pages count for more than I gave them credit for. That page now carries a dated correction too.`,`[Most GEO advice is untestable](/testable-geo-playbook/): **held.** The variance research and the survey of 45 GEO studies point the same way: measure rates, and distrust any single before-and-after.`,`[A wrong description is worse than no mention](/how-ai-describes-your-brand/): **held, with higher stakes.** Most software buyers now say they often get inaccurate AI recommendations, and at least one court has treated an AI summary as the search engine's own statement.`,`[Agents are your website's third audience](/agent-readiness-third-audience/): **half right.** Agents already use software at scale: Honeycomb [reports](https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2026-07-28/) that nearly 20% of its monthly interactive queries now come from agents. They are not yet buying. The protocols survived the year; ChatGPT's in-chat checkout did not.`]},{type:`heading`,text:`Five predictions for year two`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Here are 5 predictions I expect to be graded on. I'll come back to them next September.`},{type:`bullets`,items:[`**Another source will move, and not because of quality.** At least one major engine will sharply change which sources it cites because of a licensing, retrieval, or cost decision, and independent trackers will see it within days.`,`**Visibility data will be free, and budgets will follow outcomes.** Google Search Console and Bing Webmaster Tools already report AI impressions and citations at no cost. The paid value in AEO will move to accuracy, recommendation, and evidence that a specific action changed a specific answer.`,`**Paid and organic answers will be reported separately.** With ads running in ChatGPT and in Google's AI Overviews, marketing teams will split organic answer share from sponsored placements, the way SEO and SEM split two decades ago.`,`**Agents will read first and buy later.** Agent visits will become a line item B2B teams report for their own sites, while purchases completed entirely inside an AI chat stay a small share of B2B buying.`,`**Brand context will become infrastructure.** A governed store of approved facts, claims, and proof, with versions and approvals, will become a standard part of AEO and content platforms, and buyers will ask vendors exactly how their before-and-after numbers were measured.`]},{type:`paragraph`,text:`For our part, year two means more of the same discipline: measure the answers buyers actually see, engine by engine; make one Corporate Context the source for every output, whether the first reader is a person or a model; let agents take on more of the execution, with a person approving what ships; and keep verification honest.`},{type:`heading`,text:`The facts have to hold`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Gwen ended her note with a promise: your brand, AI ready. From where I sit, that means something specific. Your brand is found, understood accurately, and chosen, by the engines and by the buyers they send. The models will keep changing every few months. Our job is to make sure the facts hold.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Thank you to Gwen and Jia for a year of building together, and to every customer who told us when a number didn't look right. Those conversations made the product more rigorous.`}]},{slug:`your-brand-ai-ready-one-year-of-solcrys`,ogImage:`/og/news-your-brand-ai-ready-one-year-of-solcrys.png`,kind:`founder-note`,title:`Your Brand, AI Ready: One Year of SolCrys`,metaTitle:`Your Brand, AI Ready: One Year of SolCrys`,description:`SolCrys co-founder and CEO Gwen Chen on year one — why AI is now the first reader of your brand, and how one Corporate Context came to power AI answers, webpages, booth animation, and community engagement for the teams we work with.`,date:`2026-09-22`,datePublished:`2026-09-22`,updated:`2026-09-22`,tag:`Year One`,author:{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys AI`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`},relatedSlugs:[`a-year-in-view-the-facts-had-to-hold`,`introducing-the-corporate-context-engine`,`introducing-solcrys-aeo`],body:[{type:`paragraph`,text:`I started my marketing career in SEO in 2007, helping a Chinese building materials startup outrank incumbents many times its size. It was my first lesson that a small team with a sharper understanding of how discovery works can beat a bigger budget. From there I moved into communications and spent five years as a journalist interviewing executives, officials, traders, and analysts, which taught me to ask better questions and to tell the difference between a claim and a fact.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Then I came to the United States, where I have now worked for ten years. I began as a growth hacker at AppSheet and branched into product marketing and GTM strategy in 2019, and along the way I did nearly everything a marketer is asked to do: SEO and SEM, case studies, running a blog site, events strategy, partner training, sales enablement, product launches, category marketing, and analyst relations.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`My scope kept changing, but the pressures never did. There was always more to do than any team could take on, so every planning cycle began with what marketing should prioritize. Leadership always wanted proof that the work mattered, so every review came back to how we measure success. Every launch set the bar for the next one, so every retrospective asked how we get better with each cycle. And behind all of it sat the question that matters most, which is how we serve customers well.`},{type:`heading`,text:`AI changed both sides of marketing at once`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Those questions matter more today than they ever have, because AI has changed both sides of marketing at once. On one side, Claude and ChatGPT can now produce in seconds what used to take a team weeks. On the other, buyers have started turning to those same engines to decide what to buy and whom to trust, which means AI is now the first reader of your brand and often the first voice to describe it. More content does not prepare a brand for that moment. Great work has never been about quantity; it has always been about quality, and no matter how powerful the models become, they cannot make a brand ready on their own. Readiness comes from knowing what is true about your company, what deserves to be communicated, and how to tell it, in the right format and through the right channel, to reach your audience.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Three of us said yes on September 18`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That conviction is why, on September 18, 2025, three of us decided to work together. Jia, Eason, and I set out to build agentic marketing solutions on that foundation, so that any brand could be ready for a world where AI stands between it and its customers.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`We began the way I was trained to begin, by listening. Our first months were spent on customer insights, sitting in interviews and deep dives with marketing leaders, and those conversations kept circling back to the same blind spot. Buyers had started asking AI what to buy, whom to trust, and which company leads a category, while the brands being discussed had no idea what AI was saying about them.`},{type:`heading`,text:`A year of shipping, driven by what customers asked for`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That blind spot became our first major product. In May 2026, shortly after Raejeanne Skillern, former CMO of AWS, joined us as Strategic Advisor, we launched SolCrys AEO as a governed loop that lets a brand measure its visibility, diagnose the gaps, execute the next best action, and verify what changed. We were deliberate about building a loop and not another dashboard, because a dashboard tells you where you stand while a loop helps you improve.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`MCP came next, because customers wanted to use SolCrys without leaving the environments where they already write, code, analyze, and create. We shipped it in June and watched teams turn visibility insights straight into content. Once they could see and act on how they appeared in AI answers, they wanted to know whether the answers were right, and whether being mentioned meant being chosen. In July we answered both, first with Answer Accuracy, which flags ungrounded claims and helps teams correct them, and then with Recommendation, which measures how favorably AI endorses a brand compared with its competitors. We closed the month by launching Corporate Context Engine, a living record of a company's approved facts, claims, and proof, alongside Community Intelligence, which starts with Reddit and helps teams join the conversations that shape AI answers. In August we added Signals, which monitors a customer's industry and competitors and briefs them daily so that what they publish keeps them cited. And in September we launched our web product and our animation product at AI Infra Summit, together with our design partner Cornelis.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Cornelis: three requests, one context`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Looking back, none of this came from a roadmap we dreamed up in a room. Our portfolio has been driven by customer needs, and specifically by needs we heard again and again across a broad set of customers. [Our work with Cornelis](/customers/cornelis/) shows how that happens in practice. They started working with us on AEO, then asked us to help with their webpages, and then brought us in to create the booth animation for their presence at the summit. Each request looked like a separate project at first, but every one of them drew on the same approved facts, the same claims, and the same proof.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Expanding from core AEO into webpages and animation may look like a stretch, but to us it was the natural next step. Writing copy for AI does not happen in a vacuum. The facts an AI engine cites are the same facts a buyer reads on your website and hears at your booth, and a good marketing experience meets customers wherever they are, whether those customers are AI or human, whether they are browsing the web or showing up in person.`},{type:`heading`,text:`One system, not two playbooks`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Cornelis is not an exception. Across our customers, marketing teams are finding that AI answers, webpages, community conversations, and event content can no longer be run as separate projects, because buyers and AI engines read them all as one story. What these teams need is one system: a single Corporate Context, built once, from which agents and humans work together to create every output. Today that context powers AI answers, webpages, booth animation, community engagement, and industry and competitor monitoring for the teams we work with. This is the discipline I spent nearly twenty years practicing by hand, now built into the way our customers' work gets done.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`It would be tempting to handle all of this by running two playbooks, one for people and one for AI, but marketing to humans and to AI separately is not a good strategy. Your customers move between the two without noticing the seam. They read a Reddit thread, ask ChatGPT to compare options, visit your website, and stop by your booth, and they expect the same story at every stop. That is why we build everything from one context, so the story holds together whether the first reader is a person or a model.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Your brand, AI ready`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That is what we mean by your brand, AI ready. You do what you are good at, building great products and serving your customers, and we make sure your brand shows up accurately and consistently wherever those customers look, whether in an AI answer, on a webpage, or at your booth.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`To Jia and Eason, thank you for saying yes on September 18. To our customers and advisors, thank you for every piece of feedback, because every feature we shipped began with something you told us. We built year one by listening, and we intend to build year two the same way.`}]},{slug:`introducing-the-corporate-context-engine`,ogImage:`/og/news-introducing-the-corporate-context-engine.png`,kind:`founder-note`,title:`Announcing the SolCrys Corporate Context Engine`,metaTitle:`Announcing the SolCrys Corporate Context Engine | SolCrys`,description:`Today we're introducing the SolCrys Corporate Context Engine — a living record of your company that keeps AI current as your business evolves.`,date:`2026-07-28`,datePublished:`2026-07-28`,updated:`2026-07-28`,tag:`Launch`,author:{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`},leadParagraph:`A month ago, we launched SolCrys Answer Accuracy to help companies catch and correct what AI says about their brands.`,relatedSlugs:[`introducing-solcrys-aeo`,`why-prompt-pulse-and-the-chatgpt-tracker-are-free`],body:[{type:`paragraph`,text:`Today's buyers don't search the way they used to. They ask ChatGPT, Gemini, Google AI Overview, Claude, Perplexity, and other AI engines, and those answers increasingly determine which vendors make the shortlist. The challenge is that AI doesn't always have the latest facts. It may describe an outdated product, repeat inaccurate third-party claims, or miss your latest positioning altogether. Even if your website is current, AI may still rely on information published months or years ago.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`SolCrys Answer Accuracy helps marketing teams stay ahead of those mistakes. It continuously monitors what AI engines say about your company, flags inaccurate or unsupported claims, recommends actions to correct them, and verifies whether those corrections actually change future AI answers. For the first time, teams can measure the gap between what their company knows to be true and what AI tells their buyers.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`As customers adopted Answer Accuracy, they started asking a bigger question.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Accuracy isn't something you fix once`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The problem wasn't correcting AI once—it was keeping AI current as the business evolved.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Customers launch products, update pricing, refine messaging, enter new markets, and respond to competitors. Each change affects what AI should say about their company, meaning an accurate answer today can become outdated tomorrow.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The approved information AI needs is rarely in one place. It's scattered across websites, documentation pages, launch announcements, and sales decks.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Answer Accuracy showed customers where AI was wrong. The next question became obvious: how do you continuously keep AI aligned with your business?`},{type:`heading`,text:`Introducing the Corporate Context Engine`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Today, we're introducing the Corporate Context Engine.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Give SolCrys a product URL, and it builds a living record of your company. Instead of relying on scattered websites, presentations, or docs, your team gets a single source of truth for your products, messaging, market, and competitors.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`As your business changes, your Corporate Context changes with it. Upload a new deck, pricing update, messaging brief, competitor announcement, or any PDF, PowerPoint, Word document, CSV, or image, and SolCrys refreshes that record automatically. There's no need to re-brief every team or start over. The latest approved context is available everywhere it is needed.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That context powers every workflow in SolCrys. Teams can understand why AI is getting their brand wrong, generate content grounded in approved messaging, and measure whether AI answers improve over time.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Several customers are already piloting the Corporate Context Engine. They're using it to keep product updates, positioning changes, and other critical company knowledge current. Some measure whether new messaging is appearing in AI-generated answers. Others track how quickly AI stops mentioning a retired product after a major website redesign. One customer uses the Engine to monitor how AI describes its products during a product recall, ensuring customers receive the latest approved information.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Introducing Community Intelligence`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`We're also launching SolCrys Community Intelligence, a new product line for the communities where buyers research, starting with Reddit.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Reddit has become one of the web's most influential sources of product research. Buyers ask questions, compare vendors, and share real-world experiences—and those conversations increasingly influence how AI answers similar questions.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Community Intelligence continuously monitors Reddit for discussions about your brand, products, competitors, and category. It identifies feature requests, misconceptions, competitive comparisons, and other conversations that matter most.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`When a discussion needs attention, SolCrys drafts a response grounded in your approved Corporate Context—your product facts, messaging, and writing guide—so your team can respond quickly and consistently. You can publish the response as generated or review and edit it before posting, giving your team full control over every interaction.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The result is a closed loop: identify the conversations shaping AI, participate with accurate information, and measure how those changes influence future AI answers.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Reddit is where Community Intelligence starts, not where it stops. Over time it will extend to Hacker News, G2, TrustRadius, and the other communities that shape how buyers evaluate your category.`},{type:`heading`,text:`One foundation for every workflow`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The Corporate Context Engine sits underneath the entire SolCrys platform. It captures the facts about your products, market, competitors, messaging, and writing guide, then powers Answer Accuracy, Deep Analysis, Recommended Actions, Community Intelligence, and every downstream workflow built on top of them.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`This is just the beginning. Our vision is a Corporate Context Engine that becomes the foundation for every marketing workflow in SolCrys.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Answer Accuracy tells you where AI gets your brand wrong. The Corporate Context Engine keeps the ground truth current, so AI can keep pace as your business evolves.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Ground truth, on demand.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Availability`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The Corporate Context Engine and Community Intelligence are both available on the Custom plan.`},{type:`cta`,text:`See Custom plan pricing`,href:`https://app.solcrys.com/pricing`}]},{slug:`why-prompt-pulse-and-the-chatgpt-tracker-are-free`,ogImage:`/og/news-why-prompt-pulse-and-the-chatgpt-tracker-are-free.png`,kind:`founder-note`,title:`Why we made Prompt Pulse and the ChatGPT Visibility Tracker free`,metaTitle:`Why our AI visibility tools are free | A founder note from Eason Wang`,description:`A note from SolCrys co-founder Eason Wang on why we made two tools — the free ChatGPT Visibility Tracker and Prompt Pulse — free for any brand: seeing where you stand in AI search shouldn't be locked behind enterprise pricing.`,dek:`We made two tools free — the ChatGPT Visibility Tracker and Prompt Pulse. A note on why seeing the problem shouldn't cost what fixing it does.`,date:`2026-06-03`,datePublished:`2026-06-03`,updated:`2026-06-03`,tag:`Founder Note`,author:{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`},leadParagraph:`AI search is becoming the front door to your brand. Buyers ask ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini, and Google AI Overviews the questions they used to type into Google, and the answer comes back naming a short list of brands. The hard part isn't improving your spot in that answer — it's that most teams have no way to even see it.`,body:[{type:`paragraph`,text:`When we sit down with marketing and brand teams, the first reaction is almost always the same: curiosity mixed with a little dread. They suspect AI engines are already shaping how buyers find them, but they can't tell whether ChatGPT recommends them, ignores them, or names a competitor. And when they go looking for the data, they hit a wall — the tools that can show them are priced for enterprises with five-figure budgets.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That bothered me. You shouldn't have to sign a five-figure contract to find out whether AI search is quietly routing your buyers to a competitor. Seeing the problem should be free. So we built two tools and gave them away.`},{type:`heading`,text:`The free ChatGPT Visibility Tracker`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Enter your domain, and we run the high-intent prompts your buyers actually ask ChatGPT — then show you where you land: mentioned, cited, or skipped, and which competitor gets named in your place. No credit card, results in about five minutes. It answers one question honestly: when someone asks ChatGPT about your category, are you in the room?`},{type:`cta`,text:`Run the free ChatGPT Visibility Tracker`,href:`/free-chatgpt-visibility-tracker/`},{type:`heading`,text:`Prompt Pulse`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The tracker tells you how you're doing on the prompts that matter. Prompt Pulse tells you which prompts matter. It's free AI-demand data: the real questions buyers ask ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Google AI Overviews, organized by industry, ranked by demand, and flagged for what's rising. We built it because "what should we even be tracking?" is the question that stalls most AEO programs before they start — and the answer was buried inside enterprise data products. Now it's a page you can read for free.`},{type:`cta`,text:`Explore Prompt Pulse`,href:`/prompt-pulse/`},{type:`heading`,text:`Why we made them free`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Here's the conviction behind both. We don't think you should pay to see the problem. You should pay — if you choose to — to fix it. The diagnosis (where do I stand, what is my market actually asking AI) should be open. The execution — turning those gaps into governed, on-brand content and citations your team can approve, ship, and verify — is the hard, ongoing work, and that's what SolCrys charges for.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`There's a selfish reason too, and I'd rather be honest about it. We're a young company in a category that's still being defined. The more brands that can see what's happening to them in AI search, the faster the whole category grows up — and the better the conversations we get to have. Free tools are how we earn the right to those conversations. We would rather be the team that helped you see clearly than the one that hid the mirror behind a sales call.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`We also run both tools on ourselves, constantly. Like most brands new to AI search, we have our own gaps to close — and using the same instruments we hand to customers keeps us honest about what actually moves an answer.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Try them`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Run the tracker on your domain, then open Prompt Pulse for your industry. If what you see makes you want to do something about it, that's what the rest of SolCrys is for. And if it just makes the shift to AI search a little less of a black box, that's a good outcome too.`}],relatedSlugs:[`introducing-solcrys-aeo`,`welcoming-raejeanne-skillern`]},{slug:`introducing-solcrys-aeo`,ogImage:`/og/news-introducing-solcrys-aeo.png`,kind:`founder-note`,title:`Introducing SolCrys AEO — the operating platform for brands to win AI search`,metaTitle:`Introducing SolCrys AEO | A note from the founders`,description:`Today we're launching SolCrys AEO — the operating platform for brands to measure, diagnose, fix, and verify their visibility across ChatGPT, Perplexity, Google AI Overview, Gemini, and the rest of the AI answer layer.`,dek:`Today we're launching SolCrys AEO. Measure visibility. Diagnose gaps. Activate the right actions. Verify what moved. All across leading answer engines, from one workspace governed by your brand.`,date:`2026-05-20`,datePublished:`2026-05-20`,updated:`2026-05-20`,tag:`Launch`,leadParagraph:`Brand discovery is expanding beyond traditional search engines into AI search engines. Buyers now ask ChatGPT, Perplexity, Gemini, and Google AI Overview the questions they once typed into Google.`,body:[{type:`paragraph`,text:`For marketing teams, the impact is immediate. CMOs are building AEO strategy. SEO teams have added AEO to their scope. Campaign teams want their sponsored thought-leadership articles to surface in AI engines.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`But moving fast is hard. Teams are already using AI tools to draft content, SEO platforms to track rankings, and social listening tools to measure share of voice. None of these were built for AEO — and rolling out another tool across SEO, PMM, PR, brand, and communications can become a project of its own.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`What teams want is simpler: one place to measure their brand across every AI answer engine, find the fixes that move the needle, and ship brand-consistent content.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Introducing SolCrys AEO`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Today we're launching SolCrys AEO — the operating platform for brands to win AI search. Measure visibility. Diagnose gaps. Activate the right actions. Verify impact. All across leading answer engines, from one workspace governed by your brand tone and voice.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Industry-leading engine coverage`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`SolCrys covers the engines that matter most today: ChatGPT, Perplexity, Google AI Overview, and Gemini. Additional engines, including shopping and retail, are available through selected plans.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Brand-safe and managed`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`SolCrys is fully managed. No infrastructure to stand up and maintain. The Corporate Context Layer keeps your messaging framework, brand writing guide, and approved claims governed and secured — forming the operating boundaries for every action SolCrys recommends.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`One tool for every team and product line`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Whether you're a lean team or a multi-product enterprise, SolCrys adapts to how your organization works. For smaller organizations, SolCrys brings the full AEO workflow into one focused workspace — so teams can move faster without adding operational complexity. For larger organizations, dedicated workspaces can be created by product line or function — each grounded in its own messaging, claims, and product truth.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Seamless workflow integration`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`SolCrys is built on the Model Context Protocol (MCP) — the open standard for connecting AI systems to the tools you already use. That means SolCrys plugs into your marketing stack natively: content management systems, AI writing and coding tools such as Claude Cowork, ChatGPT, and Codex. As more systems expose MCP servers, new integrations come online without custom integration work.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Product deep dive`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`SolCrys AEO brings the full AEO workflow into one platform — from tracking visibility to finding gaps, shipping fixes, and proving impact.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Track the prompts that matter`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`You can't defend what you can't measure. SolCrys shows where your brand stands across leading answer engines — from mention rate and share of voice to citations and sentiment — tied to the prompts that matter most. Brand, PR, PMM, and campaign teams get one clear view your CMO can take to the board.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Find the gaps and ship the fixes`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`See which prompts your brand wins and where it's lagging. SolCrys identifies the top domains driving answers, shows where your brand is mentioned and why, then recommends actions to move the needle — from a content update or UGC strategy to a PR angle. Run content audits and manage the work in a kanban view with owners and status tracked across teams.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`Verify what moved`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`After each action ships, re-run the same prompt set to measure what changed. SolCrys tracks citation rate, AI answer accuracy, recommendation share, and visibility movement, so teams can see which fixes improved the answer and which gaps still need work. Every result ties back to the original prompt, citation, and action — giving teams a clear way to prove impact.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Try it today`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The fastest way to see how AI engines are talking about your brand is to run a free audit. We'll surface where you're already winning, where you're invisible, and which fixes will move the needle first.`},{type:`cta`,text:`Start your free audit`,href:`https://app.solcrys.com/audit`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`If you'd rather start with the methodology, our resource library covers AEO fundamentals, per-engine optimization playbooks, and the buyer guides we publish for marketing teams evaluating this category.`},{type:`signatures`,authors:[{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`},{name:`Eason Wang`,role:`Co-Founder & CPO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/eason-wang/`},{name:`Jia Chang`,role:`Co-Founder & CTO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/jia-c/`}]}],relatedSlugs:[`raejeanne-skillern-strategic-advisor`,`welcoming-raejeanne-skillern`]},{slug:`raejeanne-skillern-strategic-advisor`,ogImage:`/og/news-raejeanne-skillern-strategic-advisor.png`,kind:`press-release`,title:`SolCrys Names Raejeanne Skillern, Former AWS CMO and Intel Leader, as Strategic Advisor`,metaTitle:`SolCrys Names Raejeanne Skillern as Strategic Advisor | SolCrys News`,description:`Veteran tech executive Raejeanne Skillern, former AWS CMO and longtime Intel data center leader, joins SolCrys as a strategic advisor as the company builds the AEO operating system for brands in AI search.`,dek:`Veteran tech executive joins SolCrys to help brands get discovered and recommended in AI search.`,date:`2026-05-13`,datePublished:`2026-05-13`,updated:`2026-05-13`,tag:`Company`,heroImage:{type:`person`,name:`Raejeanne Skillern`,role:`Strategic Advisor, SolCrys`,alt:`Portrait of Raejeanne Skillern, Strategic Advisor at SolCrys`},leadParagraph:`SolCrys, the AEO operating system for brands to win discovery in AI, today announced that Raejeanne Skillern — former Chief Marketing Officer at Amazon Web Services and a longtime hyperscale infrastructure leader — has joined the company as a strategic advisor.`,body:[{type:`paragraph`,text:`Skillern brings more than 30 years of leadership across cloud, data center infrastructure, communications, and artificial intelligence. She has led multi-billion-dollar businesses at AWS, Intel, and Flex. Skillern currently serves on the boards of Jabil (NYSE: JBL) and Dycom Industries (NYSE: DY).`},{type:`quote`,text:`Raejeanne brings a rare combination of operating experience, market insight, and board-level perspective at a moment when AI is reshaping how companies build and grow their brands. As organizations across industries adapt to AI-driven discovery and decision-making, her experience scaling global businesses and guiding technology transformation will be invaluable to SolCrys and the customers we serve.`,attribution:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Founder and CEO, SolCrys`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`At AWS, Skillern led the global marketing organization, helping to modernize marketing for AI. At Flex, she ran a multi-billion P&L as President of the Communications, Enterprise & Cloud Division, repositioning the business for cloud, optical, and 5G. Over 25 years at Intel, she built the hyperscale data center business alongside the world's leading cloud providers.`},{type:`quote`,text:`AI answer engines are changing how customers discover, evaluate, and choose companies, but most organizations are not yet equipped to manage their presence in this new environment. SolCrys gives companies a clear way to measure how they appear across AI platforms, identify what needs improvement, strengthen their visibility, and track results — while staying true to their brand. SolCrys is building the category-defining platform for how companies manage brand visibility and trust in the AI era.`,attribution:`Raejeanne Skillern`,role:`Strategic Advisor, SolCrys`},{type:`heading`,text:`About SolCrys`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`SolCrys was founded by a team of AI, data, search, marketing, and growth veterans from Amazon, Microsoft, Google, Alibaba, and Tencent, building the AEO operating system for the next era of brand discovery. The company today serves brands across HPC, AI, consumer goods, retail, and beyond.`}],relatedSlugs:[`introducing-solcrys-aeo`,`welcoming-raejeanne-skillern`]},{slug:`welcoming-raejeanne-skillern`,ogImage:`/og/news-welcoming-raejeanne-skillern.png`,kind:`founder-note`,title:`Welcoming Raejeanne Skillern to SolCrys`,metaTitle:`Welcoming Raejeanne Skillern to SolCrys | Founder Note`,description:`A note from SolCrys CEO Gwen Chen on why Raejeanne Skillern — former AWS CMO and longtime Intel hyperscale leader — is joining SolCrys as a strategic advisor, and what it signals about where the company is headed.`,dek:`A note from our founder on why Raejeanne is joining — and what it signals about where the AEO category, and SolCrys, are headed.`,date:`2026-05-13`,datePublished:`2026-05-13`,updated:`2026-05-13`,tag:`Founder Note`,author:{name:`Gwen Chen`,role:`Co-Founder & CEO, SolCrys`,linkedin:`https://www.linkedin.com/in/gwenchenx/`},heroImage:{type:`person`,name:`Raejeanne Skillern`,role:`Strategic Advisor, SolCrys`,alt:`Raejeanne Skillern, newly joined SolCrys strategic advisor`},leadParagraph:`When we started SolCrys, we made a bet that AI answer engines would fundamentally change how customers discover, evaluate, and choose brands — and that most marketing teams weren't equipped for that shift yet.`,body:[{type:`paragraph`,text:`Nine months in, that bet is no longer speculative. Buyers are starting product research in ChatGPT instead of Google. Shoppers are getting recommendations from Amazon Alexa for Shopping and Walmart Sparky before they see a listing. Google AI Mode is changing search from a list of links into AI-generated answers that increasingly shape how customers discover brands. The companies winning are the ones treating AI visibility as something they can measure, diagnose, fix, and prove — not a black box.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`That is the operating system we are building at SolCrys. And today, I'm thrilled to share that Raejeanne Skillern is joining us as a strategic advisor.`},{type:`heading`,text:`Why Raejeanne, and why now`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Raejeanne spent more than 30 years inside the buildout of modern cloud infrastructure — at Intel, where she built the hyperscale data center business alongside AWS, Azure, and Google; at Flex, where she ran a multi-billion-dollar P&L for cloud, optical, and 5G; and most recently as CMO at AWS, where she led marketing through the early phase of the generative AI shift.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`She has seen, more than almost anyone in the industry, how an inflection point in infrastructure rewrites how every category gets sold. That perspective is exactly what we need right now, for three main reasons.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`1. She understands what changes when a category moves from search-led to recommendation-led discovery.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`The buyer journey for cloud compute did not simply change — the entire vendor evaluation framework changed. The same shift is now happening for consumer brands, B2B software, retail SKUs, and professional services inside the AI answer layer. Most of our customers are still operating with SEO-era playbooks. Raejeanne has lived through this kind of transition before, and she can help us help them.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`2. She has scaled marketing organizations that operate as one team alongside product and sales.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Answer Engine Optimization is not just an SEO-team problem. It requires collaboration across SEO, PMM, brand, communications, PR, product, and sales. SolCrys is built to be the connective tissue between these teams, and Raejeanne has built the operating models that make this kind of cross-functional execution work at scale.`},{type:`subheading`,text:`3. She brings board-level perspective at exactly the right time for SolCrys.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Raejeanne sits on the boards of Jabil (NYSE: JBL) and Dycom Industries (NYSE: DY). The questions boards are now asking about AI exposure — what is our visibility, where are we losing share, is this measured — are exactly the questions SolCrys is built to answer. Having someone on the team who thinks about technology adoption from both sides of the boardroom table sharpens how we build, what we prioritize, and how we define the category.`},{type:`heading`,text:`What this signals for the brands we serve`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`If you're a customer, advisor, partner, or prospect reading this: Raejeanne's involvement is a signal of where we're investing. Over the next two quarters we are doubling down on three things.`},{type:`bullets`,items:[`Cross-engine measurement depth — moving beyond mention and citation counting to true Share of Recommendation and answer-accuracy tracking, prompt-by-prompt, across ChatGPT, Gemini, Google AI Overviews / AI Mode, Perplexity, Amazon Alexa for Shopping, Walmart Sparky, and ChatGPT Shopping.`,`Corporate Context as the brand operating layer — ensuring every action SolCrys recommends is grounded in your approved stats, claims, and guardrails by default, so your content stays on-brand, accurate, and defensible.`,`The execution loop — closing the gap between 'we found the gap' and 'we fixed it', with draft briefs to media outlets, analysts and influencers, FAQ updates, and content actions tied to measurable movements in AI answers.`]},{type:`heading`,text:`Closing`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`I started SolCrys with my co-founders Eason and Jia because we believed brand discovery was about to be rewritten, and the teams building inside that change deserved a real operating system, not another dashboard.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Bringing Raejeanne onto the team will help us better serve our customers as they navigate this next phase of AI discovery.`},{type:`paragraph`,text:`Welcome, Raejeanne. We're thrilled to have you with us.`}],relatedSlugs:[`raejeanne-skillern-strategic-advisor`,`introducing-solcrys-aeo`]}]};export{e as t};